      "id": "RB-kafka-lag",
      "title": "Kafka consumer lag growing",
      "type": "runbook",
      "services": [
        "kafka",
        "accounting",
        "fraud-detection"
      ],
      "category": "queue_backlog",
      "date": null,
      "snippet": "1. Is the consumer running? CrashLoopBackOff on the consumer => fix that first. 2. Is the consumer slow because of a dependency (Postgres latency)? Fix the dependency. 3. Is the consumer itself slow per message (kafkaQueueProblems flag, CPU)? Scale consumers up to the partition count. `kubectl -n otel-demo exec deploy/kafka -- /opt/kafka/bin/kafka-consumer-groups.sh --bootstrap-server localhost:90\u2026",
      "score": 67.212
    },
    {
      "id": "PM-2026-06-17",
      "title": "Accounting consumer lag from a long-running Postgres migration",
      "type": "postmortem",
      "services": [
        "postgresql",
        "accounting",
        "product-catalog",
        "kafka"
      ],
      "category": "datastore_degradation",
      "date": "2026-06-17",
      "snippet": "## Summary A manual `ALTER TABLE` on `orders` held a lock for 40 minutes. Accounting's inserts slowed from 30ms to ~1s, and its Kafka consumer lag grew past 5,000 messages. Product-catalog reads were also slower. The alert fired on consumer lag. ## Root cause Datastore degradation in postgresql (lock contention). Accounting was healthy but blocked on the database. ## Resolution Cancelled the migra\u2026",
      "score": 30.032
    },
    {
      "id": "PM-2026-08-02",
      "title": "Currency crash loop after a release missing its data file",
      "type": "postmortem",
      "services": [
        "currency",
        "frontend",
        "checkout"
      ],
      "category": "crash_loop",
      "date": "2026-08-02",
      "snippet": "## Summary Currency 2.0.1 shipped without `currency_conversion.json`. Pods exited on start-up (`std::runtime_error`), entered CrashLoopBackOff and frontend errors reached 60% because almost every page converts prices. ## Root cause Crash loop introduced by a bad release (packaging error). ## Resolution `kubectl rollout undo deployment/currency`. ## Lessons - `waiting: CrashLoopBackOff` + near-zero\u2026",
      "score": 15.844
    },
    {
      "id": "PM-2026-01-20",
      "title": "Payment 1.7.3 release failed card charges with a TypeError",
      "type": "postmortem",
      "services": [
        "payment",
        "checkout"
      ],
      "category": "bad_deploy",
      "date": "2026-01-20",
      "snippet": "## Summary A payment release changed the shape of the `amount` object. 35% of charges failed with `TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'units')`. Checkout's error-rate alert fired 9 minutes after rollout. ## Root cause Bad deploy. The new code assumed `amount.units` was always set; older clients sent `amount.nanos` only. ## Resolution `kubectl rollout undo deployment/payment`. \u2026",
      "score": 15.404
    },
    {
      "id": "RB-crashloop",
      "title": "CrashLoopBackOff triage",
      "type": "runbook",
      "services": [
        "*"
      ],
      "category": "crash_loop",
      "date": null,
      "snippet": "1. `kubectl -n otel-demo logs <pod> --previous` shows why the last start failed. 2. Missing env var / config file / bad command -> check the most recent config change or rollout. 3. If a rollout caused it: `kubectl -n otel-demo rollout undo deployment/<svc>`. 4. If an env var changed: restore it with `kubectl set env`.",
